WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.000
Apollo: the way home. How NASA aimed at a moving Moon.
These diagrams are schematic, not to scale.

00:05.000 --> 00:10.000
Tracking informs an estimate of the spacecraft's position
and velocity at a known time: its state vector.

00:10.000 --> 00:15.000
Three position components and three velocity components
provide the starting state for predicting a path.

00:15.000 --> 00:20.000
A prograde burn adds speed and raises the far end of an orbit.
This ellipse is an ideal two-body illustration.

00:20.000 --> 00:25.000
Translunar injection changes a low Earth orbit
into an outward transfer toward the Moon.

00:25.000 --> 00:30.000
The Moon moves during the coast.
Aim for a future encounter, not its departure position.

00:30.000 --> 00:35.000
Departure, burn direction and flight time must agree.
NASA refined the solution with numerical integration.

00:35.000 --> 00:40.000
Lunar gravity bends the path. Some transfers were designed
for a free return toward Earth after passing the Moon.

00:40.000 --> 00:45.000
Apollo fired a braking burn to enter lunar orbit.
Free return and capture require different targeted paths.

00:45.000 --> 00:50.000
Tracking updates the state estimate.
Small course corrections trim the predicted miss.

00:50.000 --> 00:55.000
The return targets a narrow Earth-entry corridor.
Observe, solve, correct: the flight plan is a living solution.
